  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nConditions are favorable for at least patchy fog development early \nthis morning, however, cirrus clouds from an MCS in southwest KS \ncould prevent widespread dense fog formation. Any fog that develops \nshould burn off by 15Z and then we will be left with sunny skies and \nlight north winds as a sfc ridge moves in behind a cold front. \nTemperatures should top out around 90 degrees this afternoon with \nslightly lower dew points than the last few afternoons. Tonight, a \nmid-level shortwave will move over southwest KS. An associated \ncomplex of storms could clip central portions of the state early \ntomorrow morning, and a few CAMs show isolated storms developing \nalong a theta-e/instability gradient in central KS that may briefly \nimpact locations in and around Ottawa and Dickinson Counties.\n\nThe sfc ridge keeps conditions dry on Thursday. Temperatures will start\noff in the low 60s and warm into the mid to upper 80s during the \nafternoon. Winds will again be light throughout the day. Similar \nconditions are expected on Friday as the ridge moves east and a sfc \ntrough deepens in the Front Range. Stronger southerly flow and an \nexpanding mid-level ridge over the area will allow temperatures to \nquickly warm this weekend. Highs will be well into the 90s Saturday \nand Sunday with heat index values around 100 degrees. South- \nsouthwest winds will be breezy, gusting 25 to 30 mph each afternoon.\n\nThe probability for thunderstorms will also increase late this \nweekend, especially in far northern and north-central KS with a sfc \nboundary draped across the area. The boundary looks to stay\npositioned across northern Kansas and southeastern Nebraska through \nthe first half of next week, which will keep at least low chance \nPOPs in the forecast each afternoon/evening. Temperatures also stay \nhot moving into the start of September.",
